A||N||7||Seanh||Changing Interior Color||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||07-18-2000||05:29 PM||||I was wanting to redo the interior of my 86 with some different colors. I can find the Vinal paint in the autoparts store. Its this what I should use? And, from folks who have done this already, what does the finished product look and feel like, does it feel like smooth paint, or does it retain the feel of vinal? Also, how durable is it? Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||07-18-2000||06:28 PM||||You can go to an auto paint place and get interior paint for your car. When I did mine I asked them at the store and they fixed me up.  A little paint goes a long way. I got some of the stock grey to paint the  replacement plastic seat spring/seat belt retaining covers.  The replacement ones come only in tan.<P>Using a simple pressurized air sprayer, I was able to paint it so that it looked like new.  I even touched up some dash blemishes.<P>The texture looked the same as the original plastic.  You need some skill so as not to lay down a heavy coat.<P>If you are doing the dash and a lot of other stuff, you may want to remove them from the car.  Masking correctly is an art by itself.<P>If the painted surface doesn't get too much abuse, it should hold up.  Looks like vinyl because the paint I used was for vinyl.||||reg||3
Z||000002||Seanh||07-18-2000||08:03 PM||||Thanks, I was going to use spray can vinyl paint from Advanced Auto Parts. But now I got to thinking that I should use my paint sprayer that hooks to the air compressor. I was planning on doing the whole interior. And I am going to remove it all. I was just mainly concerned with what the vinyl feels like after its painted and clear coated. On my car, the only parts that are vinyl are the doors and the housing between the seats. The rest it plastic. Does the vinyl still feel like normal vinyl after you lay the coats on it? I was told I should use about 4 coats and then clear coat it. <P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||07-18-2000||09:51 PM||||I never did a clear coat.  Since I did not change color on the dash, I put on two real light coats.  The seat belt retainers I just did two light coats.  They now have some dings in them from the seat belt clasp hitting them, but one has a broken male piece that fits into the seat bracket.  So thats ready to be replaced.  So I'll have to dig out the can of paint. <P>It is a pain to remove the seats, just to take off that cover.  Maybe when I get a wave of enthusiasm.||||reg||1
Z||000004||My7Fieros||07-19-2000||02:44 AM||||I repainted the interior in my Formula....well, parts of it. I was going to go w/ the mixed paint and the whole spray rig setup, but decided (against my better judgement) to go w/ the spray can stuff. Man did it come out looking fantastic!<BR>I cant think of the name brand of the paint I used, but I got it at Colormatch Automotive Paint (theres your plug Chuck..lol). I painted the entire center console, from the aux. guage pod all the way back to the storage comp. between the seats. I also painted the gauge pod surround, as well as the top of the dash. The 88's had the solid darker tan color, and I like the two tone that I have in my other Fieros (86's and 87's). I pulled the pieces, scuffed them pretty good, and shot the paint on. Looks great! And has help up beautifully! I got a golden brownish color to offset the solid tan in the car.<BR>One thing you may want to remember though, is that seatbelts are all together a diff. story....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000005||DXR_DAD||07-19-2000||04:48 PM||||OK all this talk about paint/dye!!!!!! new looking interiors, re-glue, fix up,<BR>I'm gonna do it   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> will post pics soon<P>Be Like NIKE&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;"Just Do It"  !!!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Thanks for all the info guys, even though I did not start this post. Like always I follow<BR>along and pick up on some good stuff.<P><BR>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=99075&a=723593&p=24416340&Sequence=2"> <p>[This message has been edited by DXR_DAD (edited 07-19-2000).]||||reg||14
Z||000006||rogergarrison||07-19-2000||10:29 PM||||there was another thread on this. Go to an auto paint store, not an auto part store for the correct stuff. Just tell them interior color and theyll know what you mean. The best brand if you can find it is called 'Mar Hyde'. Its good on all parts, vinyl or hard plastic. holds up very well. dont put on clear topcoat-its recommended only for coloring seats-itll leave parts glossy and reflect in the windows. You can make it either flat or semi gloss. I use semi. Only put on enough to cover it good. Its usually prethinned and a quart will do the interior of a Fiero. Dont use the spray cans if you can help it. with and air compressor you can do a much better job. When your done it looks as good as when brand new. Be sure to clean all parts really good first. If its ever had armorall or dressing on any parts, your going to have to clean with detergent and scrub brush. If paint 'fish eyes' you can pick up a small vial at paint store called Fish Eye Eliminator so paint will cover OK (ONLY A COUPLE OF DROPS). Youll really like your 'new' interior when your done <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||DXR_DAD||07-20-2000||02:06 AM||||Thanks again guys for all the info.<BR>I kinda feel sorry for the fiero owners<BR>that don't know about this forum!! they miss<BR>out on a lot of goood stuff<P><BR>DXR_DAD||||reg||10
